WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.422 --> 00:00:05.130
Colin and I are going to the visually act

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:08.060
out a function that adds two numbers together.

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:10.450
The script calls a function with the following line-- y

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:14.310
equals MyAdd parentheses x comma y.

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:16.440
The script needs to get these values of x and y

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:19.070
and give those to the function myAdd.

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:23.520
Notice the script is not handing the variables x and y to myAdd.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:27.060
It just passes the values that were stored in x and y.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.200
MyAdd now does something with those numbers.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:32.890
The script has no idea what the function is actually doing with the number.

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:35.490
When the function is done, it can return values.

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:38.140
In this case, the function returns a single number,

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:40.440
and the script stores the number that the function

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.049
returned in the variable y.

00:00:42.049 --> 00:00:42.840
And now we&amp;#39;re done.

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:48.980
Now let&amp;#39;s go through this again in greater detail.

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:52.070
The script is going to hand two values to the function myAdd.

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:54.960
Let&amp;#39;s peek in on what the function is actually doing.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:59.545
The function is declared with the line function x equals MyAdd parentheses

00:00:59.545 --> 00:01:01.420
x comma z.

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:03.170
MyAdd is the name of the function.

00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:06.860
It takes two inputs, which it stores in x and z.

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:08.800
MyAdd gives one output.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:12.139
The script will take the output and store its value in y.

00:01:12.139 --> 00:01:14.180
Even though the function has a variable called x,

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:17.120
it is not the same x as in the script.

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:19.800
We say these two x&amp;#39;s have different scopes.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:23.920
The function&amp;#39;s x and the script&amp;#39;s x can have different values.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:26.540
The script cannot change the value of x in the function.

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:28.920
It can only pass a value to the function,

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:31.940
and the function will decide what to do with it.

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:34.907
MyAdd now stores this second value in z.

00:01:34.907 --> 00:01:37.240
The function takes these two values, adds them together,

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:39.480
and stores them back in the variable x.

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:41.640
Notice that the variable x inside the function

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:45.090
has a different value than the variable x outside the function.

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:49.400
This is possible because the two variables are not of the same scope.

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:51.950
Now there are no more lines of code in the function.

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:55.860
It returns a value-- 3 in this case-- to the script that called it.

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:57.500
The script stores this value in y.

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:04.430
In theory, the myAdd function can take any two inputs.

00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:08.630
In this case, the script is passing the letter Q and the number 3.

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:11.020
When the function tries to add the values together,

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:14.690
it will return a number, but it doesn&amp;#39;t necessarily make sense.

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:19.710
Because of this, it&amp;#39;s a good idea to have your function

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:23.640
check to see what the inputs are and make sure that they are what you want.

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:26.720
If not, the function should return an error.

